          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Power Camping Actually Needs
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Add Up What You Will Run</h3>

          <p>
            The single most useful thing you can do before buying a portable power station for camping is add up what you will actually run. Most people overestimate it. A weekend of phones, LED lights and a fan uses less than 150Wh, which a 250Wh unit handles with room to spare. The number only climbs once you add a 12V fridge, a CPAP with a heated humidifier, Starlink or any kind of electric cooking, and it climbs fast.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Work in Watt-Hours</h3>

          <p>
            Work in watt-hours, not watts. Watts tell you whether the station can start and run a device; watt-hours tell you how long. Multiply each device's average draw by the hours you use it per day, add them up, then divide by about 0.85 to account for inverter and conversion losses. That gives you the battery capacity you need per day. Multiply by the number of nights between recharges, and you have your target.
          </p>

