          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reading the Table</h3>

          <p>
            Read the table and the buying decision usually makes itself. Phones, lights and a fan point to a 250Wh to 300Wh unit like the Jackery Explorer 300 Plus or EcoFlow RIVER 3 Plus. Add a CPAP and you want at least 300Wh per night with the humidifier off; our guide to whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-cpap/">a portable power station can run a CPAP</a> walks through the machine-by-machine math. Add a 12V fridge and you are in 1kWh territory for anything longer than a single night.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Induction Is the Outlier</h3>

          <p>
            Induction is the outlier. A single burner at 1,500W drains a 1kWh battery in well under an hour of continuous use, and a 300W unit cannot start one at all. If you want to cook electrically, size for it explicitly or keep the propane stove. For a broader walkthrough of sizing beyond camping, see <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">what size portable power station you need</a>.
