        <div id="weight-vs-capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Weight vs Capacity: What You Can Realistically Carry
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity and Weight Move Together</h3>

          <p>
            Capacity and weight move together, and for camping that is the trade-off that matters most. LiFePO4 power stations land at roughly 28 to 40Wh per pound once you include the inverter, case and ports. That puts 250Wh to 300Wh units at 8 to 10 pounds, 1kWh units at 22 to 28 pounds and 2kWh units at 45 to 65 pounds. There is no way around the physics; a lighter unit with the same capacity is usually using older NMC cells that wear out sooner.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Weight to How You Camp</h3>

          <p>
            Match the weight to how you camp. For walk-in sites, paddle-in camping or anywhere you carry gear more than a hundred yards, stay under 10 pounds. That is the 300Wh class. For car camping, where the battery lives in the trunk and moves 20 feet to the picnic table, 25 pounds is fine, and the jump to 1kWh buys you a fridge and morning coffee. For vans and RVs, weight barely matters because the unit never moves.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Don't Overbuy for Hike-In Sites</h3>

          <p>
            Do not buy a 1kWh unit for hike-in sites on the theory that more is better. A 24-pound box is miserable to carry any distance, and you will end up leaving it in the car. Two smaller units are also an option for groups: a 300Wh box at each tent is often more useful than one large station everyone has to share.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Expandable Units</h3>

          <p>
            Expandable units soften the trade-off. The EcoFlow RIVER 3 Plus can carry its 286Wh core on short trips and add an extra battery for longer ones, while the Anker SOLIX C1000 doubles to 2,112Wh with its add-on pack. If you are unsure which way your camping will go, paying a little more for expandability is cheaper than buying twice. Our main <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station rankings</a> cover the full range of expandable units.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar-at-camp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Solar Panels at Camp: What They Really Deliver
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rated Watts vs Real Output</h3>

          <p>
            Solar is what turns a power station from a battery you drain into a portable power source that keeps going. But the number printed on the panel is a laboratory rating, not what you will see at a wooded campsite. A 200W folding panel commonly delivers 120W to 150W in clear midday sun when angled properly, and far less in shade, haze or low autumn sun. Over a full day, a good rule of thumb is four to five times the panel's rating in watt-hours in summer, and two to three times in spring and fall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a 200W Panel Puts Back</h3>

          <p>
            That means a 200W panel realistically puts back 400Wh to 900Wh a day depending on season and site. That is plenty to keep a 300Wh unit full and enough to sustain a 12V fridge on a 1kWh station in summer, but it will not keep up with a heated CPAP and a fridge in October. Plan for the shortfall, not the best case.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Placement Matters More Than Brand</h3>

          <p>
            Placement matters more than panel brand. Campsites are often shaded on purpose. Run a longer extension cable so the panel can sit in the sun while you stay in the shade, re-angle it two or three times a day to follow the sun, and keep it out of the dew overnight. Do not exceed the station's solar input rating: the Jackery Explorer 300 Plus takes 100W, the Bluetti EB3A takes 200W, the RIVER 3 Plus takes 220W and the Anker SOLIX C1000 takes 600W. Extra panel capacity above the limit is just wasted.
          </p>

          <p>
            For panel recommendations, see our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels</a>, and for wiring, connectors and series-versus-parallel setups, read <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">how to charge a portable power station with solar</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="car-charging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Charging From Your Car While You Drive
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">12V Socket Charging Is Slow</h3>

          <p>
            Every unit on this list can charge from a vehicle's 12V socket, and for road trips that is free energy you should use. The catch is speed. Most 12V sockets are fused at 10A to 15A, which limits charging to roughly 100W to 120W. Over a three-hour drive, that adds 300Wh to 360Wh, enough to refill a 300Wh unit or put a third back into a 1kWh one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Only With the Engine Running</h3>

          <p>
            Only charge while the engine is running. Leaving a power station connected to the 12V socket with the engine off can drain your starter battery overnight, and a dead battery at a remote trailhead is a far bigger problem than a dead power station. Some vehicles keep the socket live with the ignition off, so unplug it when you park.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Alternator Chargers</h3>

          <p>
            For faster charging on the move, some brands sell alternator chargers that connect directly to the vehicle's starter battery and push several hundred watts while driving. They are worth it for overlanders and van owners who drive most days, and overkill for weekend campers. If you go that route, have the install done properly with appropriate fusing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weather-Proof Charging</h3>

          <p>
            A hidden advantage of car charging is that it does not depend on weather. On a rainy trip, a daily drive to the trailhead or into town can keep a 1kWh unit topped up when the solar panel is doing almost nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="campground-rules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Campground Rules, Quiet Hours and Gas Generators
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Generator Restrictions at Campgrounds</h3>

          <p>
            This is where a portable power station for camping beats a portable generator for camping outright. Many state and national park campgrounds restrict generator use to set hours, commonly something like 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. with quiet hours overnight, and many tent loops ban generators entirely. A power station makes no exhaust and, at light loads, essentially no noise, so the rules that restrict generators generally do not apply. Check your specific campground's posted rules, but a silent battery is almost never the problem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Quiet Hours, CPAPs and Fridges</h3>

          <p>
            Quiet hours matter most for CPAP users and anyone running a fridge. A generator cannot legally run your CPAP at 2 a.m. in most campgrounds, and a fridge that stops for ten hours overnight is barely a fridge. A battery covers exactly the hours a generator cannot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Generators Still Fit</h3>

          <p>
            Generators still have a place. For RVs with a rooftop air conditioner or a long stay with heavy electric cooking, an inverter generator supplies energy a battery cannot store economically. If that is your situation, compare options in our <a href="/best-rv-generator/">best RV generator guide</a>. Many RVers run both: a generator during allowed hours to recharge, and a power station overnight.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a deeper comparison of cost, runtime and maintenance, read <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">portable power station vs generator</a>. For pure camping energy needs below 1kWh a day, the battery wins on every count except long runtime.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold-weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cold Weather and LiFePO4 Charging Limits
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">LiFePO4 Cells</h3>

          <p>
            Every unit on this list uses LiFePO4 cells, which last far longer than older NMC chemistry and are more stable. Our explainer on <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion power stations</a> covers why. But LiFePO4 has one important limitation for camping: it should not be charged below freezing. Charging lithium cells below about 32 degrees Fahrenheit can cause lithium plating, which permanently damages the battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold-Charging Protection</h3>

          <p>
            Most power stations protect themselves by refusing to charge, or charging very slowly, when the cells are too cold. That is a feature, not a fault, but it catches people out. You wake up at 25 degrees, the panel is in full sun and the station is showing zero input. It will start charging once the cells warm up, which can take hours in a cold vehicle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Discharging in the Cold</h3>

          <p>
            Discharging in the cold is less of a problem, though capacity drops. Expect noticeably less usable energy when the battery itself is below freezing. The fix is simple: keep the station inside the tent or vehicle overnight, off the cold ground, and wrapped in a jacket or sleeping bag stuff sack if temperatures are low. Its own self-heating from use helps too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Planning for Winter Camping</h3>

          <p>
            For winter camping, especially with a CPAP, size up by about 30% and plan to charge in the warmest part of the day. Some newer units include internal battery heaters for cold-weather charging. If you camp below freezing regularly, check the spec sheet for that feature before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Safety in Tents and Vehicles
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No Carbon Monoxide</h3>

          <p>
            Power stations are among the safest ways to bring electricity into a tent because they produce no carbon monoxide. That is the core difference from gas generators, which must never run inside or near a tent, vestibule or vehicle. Our guide on whether <a href="/are-portable-power-stations-safe-indoors/">portable power stations are safe indoors</a> goes into detail, but the short answer for camping is yes, with sensible precautions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep It Dry and Off the Ground</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the unit dry and off the ground. Tent floors collect condensation, and a station sitting in a puddle is asking for trouble. Put it on a small cutting board, a storage bin lid or a camp chair. Give it a few inches of space around the vents so the fan can move air, and do not bury it under sleeping bags while it is charging or running a heavy load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat in Vehicles</h3>

          <p>
            In vehicles, the main risk is heat, not fire. A dark car in summer sun can exceed the operating temperature of most batteries. Do not leave the station on the dashboard or in a closed car in full sun for hours. Strap it down so it cannot become a projectile in a hard stop, and route cables so they are not pinched by doors or seats.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Right Cords</h3>

          <p>
            Use the right cords. A cheap, undersized extension cord running from the station to a heater or cooktop can get hot. Keep high-wattage loads plugged directly into the unit. And never run a space heater or cooktop unattended in a tent. The battery is safe; a hot element next to nylon is not.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Battery inside, generator outside, always.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A power station can sit next to your sleeping bag. A gas generator must stay at least 20 feet from any tent, vehicle or opening, pointed away from where people sleep. That difference alone is why many campers switch.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="power-banks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When a Power Bank Is Enough
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Small Loads Only</h3>

          <p>
            Not every trip needs a power station. If your only loads are a phone, a headlamp and maybe a small camera, a 20,000mAh to 27,000mAh power bank for camping weighs about a pound and holds roughly 70Wh to 100Wh, enough for four to six phone charges. For backpacking and short trips, that is the right tool, and a 300Wh station would be dead weight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Line Is AC Power</h3>

          <p>
            The line is AC power. The moment you need a standard wall plug, a portable outlet for a CPAP brick, a fan or a small appliance, a power bank cannot help. Power stations also accept solar input at useful rates, while most power banks charge slowly from small panels.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carry Both</h3>

          <p>
            A good camping kit often includes both. Leave the station at camp running the CPAP and fridge, and carry a power bank on day hikes for your phone. It is cheaper than buying a larger station and keeps the station's battery for the jobs only it can do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size portable power station do I need for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For phones, lights and a fan over a weekend, 250Wh to 300Wh is enough. Add a CPAP and you want at least 300Wh per night with the humidifier off, more with it on. A 12V fridge pushes you to about 1,000Wh for two to three days. Add up your devices' watt-hours per day, divide by 0.85 and multiply by your nights between recharges.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station run a 12V camping fridge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A compressor fridge typically uses 300Wh to 600Wh per day depending on outside temperature and how often you open it. A 1kWh unit like the Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 runs one for two to three days on its own, and indefinitely in summer with a 200W solar panel. Plug it into the 12V DC port, not the AC outlet, to save energy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power station better than a generator for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most campers, yes. It is silent, makes no fumes, can run inside a tent and is allowed during quiet hours when generators are not. A gas generator still wins for running an RV air conditioner or heavy cooking over long stays. If your daily use is under about 1kWh, a power station with a solar panel is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I charge a power station from my car?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, every unit here charges from a 12V socket, typically at about 100W to 120W. A three-hour drive adds roughly 300Wh to 360Wh. Only charge with the engine running, because charging with it off can drain your starter battery. For faster charging, overlanders often add a dedicated alternator charger wired to the battery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a power station work in freezing temperatures?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It will discharge in the cold, with somewhat reduced capacity, but LiFePO4 batteries should not be charged below freezing, and most units will refuse to charge until the cells warm up. Keep the station inside your tent or vehicle overnight, off the ground, and plan to charge in the afternoon when it is warmest.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 300Wh power station run a CPAP?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With the humidifier off, most CPAP machines use 80Wh to 200Wh per night, so a 300Wh unit typically covers one to two nights. With a heated humidifier and heated tube, use can climb to 300Wh to 600Wh a night, which a 300Wh unit may not finish. Running the CPAP from a DC adapter instead of AC stretches runtime noticeably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to keep a power station inside a tent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Power stations produce no carbon monoxide or exhaust, so they are safe to use in a tent, unlike a gas generator. Keep the unit off the damp floor, leave space around its vents, and do not cover it while charging. Avoid running heating appliances unattended from it, since the hot element is the real fire risk, not the battery.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by watts instead of watt-hours</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Output watts tell you what a station can start, but watt-hours decide how long it lasts. A 1,800W unit with 300Wh of battery runs a kettle for ten minutes. Add up your daily watt-hours first, then check that the output rating covers your largest device.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running DC devices through the AC outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plugging a 12V fridge or a laptop into the AC outlet wastes 10% to 15% of your energy in conversion losses. Use the 12V DC port for fridges and fans and USB-C for laptops and phones. Over a long weekend, that efficiency can add up to an extra night of runtime.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the solar panel's rated wattage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 200W panel rarely delivers 200W at a campsite. Expect 60% to 75% of rated output in good sun, and much less in shade or autumn light. Size your panel and battery for realistic output, and move the panel during the day to follow the sun.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the battery in a cold car overnight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                LiFePO4 batteries will not charge below freezing, and a station left in a cold vehicle may refuse solar input until midday. Bring it into the tent or cab at night, keep it off the ground, and charge during the warmest hours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 1kWh unit for hike-in camping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 24-pound battery is a hardship on any carry longer than the parking lot. If you camp at walk-in or paddle-in sites, a 300Wh unit under 10 pounds will see far more use. Choose the capacity you will actually bring.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

